xnu 0299: the CPU rows follow load instead of sitting at the top

cpufreq=1 pinned all three EPSS domains to their top row (1804/2496/2995 MHz)
and nothing ever lowered them, so an idle phone still burned the top rail on
every cluster. cpufreq=2 runs a governor: each domain's row follows the busy
fraction of its cores, measured from the idle hook XNU already calls around
WFI (processor_idle in ml_processor_info_t, installed by MondrianCPU) and
sampled at 50 Hz. Load raises the row at once; decay is one row per tick, so a
quiet phone reaches the bottom row in about two seconds. cpufreq=1 still means
top and cpufreqrow=N pins row N for a one-boot experiment.

The boot command line moves to cpufreq=2 (tools/boards.py).

